Turion Jev 31B. It’s the best balance of speed, calibration and an open licence, and FP8 means a cheaper GPU. Drawbacks: it’s a first release from a small team, it was trained on only about 24,500 synthetic examples, and it doesn’t list which languages it supports.

GLiDE v1.2. It has the highest score and is fast. Drawbacks: the licence limits production use by large companies, so legal must clear it, and its slowest requests can take over a second.

d3-ultra. It has the strongest backing and the best worst-case speed, around 670 ms. It also uses the same API as d3-mini, which already scored 94.1% on your HR test, including 93.4% on Hinglish. Drawbacks: its calibration is the weakest, and it runs custom code that security should review.
